      "title": "🔓 [The New Yorker] Briefly Noted Book Reviews",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://media.newyorker.com/photos/6a8da55a96bf82fbda6ea418/master/pass/r50092.jpg\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://media.newyorker.com/photos/6a8da55a96bf82fbda6ea418/master/pass/r50092.jpg\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://media.newyorker.com/photos/6a8da55a96bf82fbda6ea418/master/pass/r50092.jpg\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://media.newyorker.com/photos/6a8da55a96bf82fbda6ea418/master/pass/r50092.jpg\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://media.newyorker.com/photos/6a8da55a96bf82fbda6ea418/master/pass/r50092.jpg\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://media.newyorker.com/photos/6a8da55a96bf82fbda6ea418/master/pass/r50092.jpg\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\n<img src=\"https://media.newyorker.com/photos/6a8da55a96bf82fbda6ea418/master/pass/r50092.jpg\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\" />\nSave this story Save this story **The Black Shield**_, by Wilbert L. Cooper (Farrar, Straus & Giroux)_. In this multilayered work, a journalist examines a Black police organization, known as the Black Shield, that emerged in Cleveland in the nineteen-forties, alongside his own parents’ membership in it. Cooper assesses the notion that Black communities are “safer and served better” when the police who surveil and discipline them are Black, too. In theory, he writes, the Black Shield offers opportunity for young people living in the economically distressed community where it is based, but he",
